---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Header from '../components/Header.tsx'
import '../index.css'
---

<Layout title="Why Phylogeny Matters - PhyloGuessr">
  <div class="game">
    <Header client:load />
    <div class="about-page">
      <h2>Why this stuff matters</h2>
      <p>
        Phylogenetics — the study of evolutionary relationships — reveals that
        all life shares a common ancestor and maps how closely every organism is
        related. Many genes involved in human disease have counterparts in
        distant relatives like <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drosophila_melanogaster"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">flies</a
        > and <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">yeast</a
        >; because they're cheap and fast to work with, scientists use them to
        study disease and test treatments.
      </p>

      <h3>Medicine and disease</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Drug discovery — phylogeny guides which organisms to test in: mice
          share many disease genes with us, so nearly every approved drug was
          tested in rodents first. <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zebrafish"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Zebrafish</a
          >, <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caenorhabditis_elegans"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>C. elegans</em></a
          >, and <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drosophila_melanogaster"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">fruit flies</a
          > are used for early screening because they breed fast and share key disease
          pathways with humans
        </li>
        <li>
          Cancer — two-thirds of genes implicated in human cancers have
          counterparts in <a
            href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6405444/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">fruit flies</a
          >. Nobel Prize-winning research on the cell cycle was done in <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/l-h-hartwell-s-yeast-a-model-808/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">yeast</a
          >, and programmed cell death was discovered in <em>C. elegans</em> worms
        </li>
        <li>
          Metagenomics — phylogenetics classifies <a
            href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/gut-bacteria-gene-complement-d/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">the thousands of microbial species</a
          > in a gut microbiome sample
        </li>
        <li>
          Comparative genomics — <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature13992"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">comparing human and mouse genomes</a
          > reveals regulatory DNA sequences that would otherwise be nearly impossible
          to identify
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Genetic tools</h3>
      <p>
        Many of our most powerful genetic tools were adapted from mechanisms
        that evolved in other organisms.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          CRISPR — a <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRISPR"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">bacterial immune system</a
          > repurposed to precisely edit human genes. The FDA <a
            href="https://www.statnews.com/2023/12/08/fda-approves-casgevy-crispr-based-medicine-for-treatment-of-sickle-cell-disease/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">approved the first CRISPR therapy</a
          > for sickle cell disease
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RNA_interference"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">RNA interference</a
          > — discovered in <em>C. elegans</em> worms (<a
            href="https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2006/press-release/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Nobel Prize 2006</a
          >), now the basis of approved drugs that silence disease-causing genes
        </li>
        <li>
          Transposon engineering — "jumping genes," first discovered by Barbara
          McClintock in corn, now harnessed to insert therapeutic genes into
          human cells. The <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41434-021-00254-w"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Sleeping Beauty transposon</a
          > was reconstructed from ancient fish genomes for use in cancer immunotherapy
        </li>
        <li>
          Ancestral reconstruction — scientists can <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nrg1324"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">resurrect ancient proteins</a
          > to study how they evolved, and engineer more stable versions for <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nbt.3677"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">drug development</a
          > and <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41929-018-0159-5"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">industrial applications</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          Convergent evolution — <a
            href="https://www.science.org/content/article/bats-and-dolphins-evolved-echolocation-same-way"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >bats and dolphins independently evolved echolocation through the
            same gene mutations</a
          >, revealing which molecular designs are truly optimal and guiding
          protein engineering and drug design
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Agriculture and environment</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Crop improvement — <a
            href="https://daily.jstor.org/can-crops-wild-relatives-save-troubled-agriculture/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">wild relatives</a
          > of wheat, rice, and tomato are crossed with cultivated varieties to introduce
          disease resistance and stress tolerance. <a
            href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13059-020-02224-8"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Pangenome projects</a
          > now sequence dozens of wild relatives, revealing genes lost during domestication
          that breeders can reintroduce
        </li>
        <li>
          Conservation — the <a
            href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2561-9"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">tuatara</a
          >, sole survivor of its order, is prioritized because it represents a
          unique evolutionary lineage
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Much of this began as basic curiosity — open-ended inquiry that produced
        breakthroughs no one anticipated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
